3978858583332528,"total":-0.1},"snow_normal":0.1},"titles":{"year":"2026 so far: 14 daily record highs, no daily record lows","records":"Record highs keep coming. Record lows have nearly stopped.","change":"Houston's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","rain":"Houston has gotten wetter"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 81\u00b0F was the 23rd warmest November 13 high (tied) of 135 since 1889, the warmest since 2025.","t_yest":"November 13: warmer than 82% of years","p_yest":"The high of <strong>81\u00b0F</strong> was the 23rd warmest November 13 high (tied) of 135 since 1889, the warmest since 2025. The normal high for the date is 73\u00b0F, and the record is 86\u00b0F (2005). The low of <strong>52\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Record highs keep coming. Record lows have nearly stopped.","p_rec":"If Houston's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Houston has set <strong class=\"sig\">23 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 72 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">161 record highs</strong>, far more than the 72 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (73). Houston's all-time high is 109\u00b0F, on September 4, 2000.","t_chg":"Houston's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 128 complete years: Houston's average low shows no clear change: \u22120.01\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.09 to +0.08) that includes zero. Houston's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.21\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.28), about +2.9\u00b0F since 1892. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.93\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.65 to +1.21) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.67\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.35 to +0.99). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 4 of 12 months. Highs rose in 11 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Houston has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Houston's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.49 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.00 to +0.98), about +6.7 inches since 1889. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 51.8 inches a year. The wettest year was 2017 (79.69 in); the driest, 1917 (17.66 in). Houston almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.1 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 14 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}